1
00:00:00,790 --> 00:00:03,890
This year, the Atlantic Ocean forgot to get angry.

2
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:05,440
Only ten storms...

3
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:07,910
and a single hurricane all season.

4
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:10,010
And on the other side of America...

5
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:11,530
the ocean was boiling.

6
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:13,890
What is going on?

7
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:16,240
This is Trend. What is everyone talking about?

8
00:00:16,810 --> 00:00:19,150
The Atlantic hurricane season starts in June,

9
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:20,790
and ends at the end of November.

10
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:24,560
In a normal year, the first hurricane is born in mid-August.

11
00:00:24,790 --> 00:00:28,480
But 2026 broke every rule.

12
00:00:28,970 --> 00:00:30,710
July went by... then August...

13
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:31,680
then September.

14
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:33,470
Not a single hurricane.

15
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:37,520
For the first time since satellites began watching the sky,

16
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:38,900
we reached October...

17
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:40,370
and the Atlantic was silent.

18
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:42,580
Scientists started to wonder:

19
00:00:42,710 --> 00:00:45,350
are we living through a season with no hurricanes at all?

20
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:48,150
Something that has happened only twice in history:

21
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:50,130
in 1907,

22
00:00:50,470 --> 00:00:52,090
and in 1914.

23
00:00:52,090 --> 00:00:54,680
Then, on October 8th,

24
00:00:54,990 --> 00:00:56,220
Isaias was born.

25
00:00:56,650 --> 00:00:57,970
The first hurricane of the season...

26
00:00:58,350 --> 00:01:02,190
and the latest first hurricane in 121 years.

27
00:01:02,190 --> 00:01:05,510
It hit the Florida coast as a Category 2,

28
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:08,430
with winds near 170 km/h,

29
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:11,460
and killed at least three people.

30
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:12,470
And after it...

31
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:17,490
silence returned. This hadn't happened since 1925:

32
00:01:17,490 --> 00:01:20,490
a whole season with just one hurricane.

33
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:22,570
So who stopped the hurricanes?

34
00:01:23,110 --> 00:01:25,110
The answer lies in the Pacific.

35
00:01:25,690 --> 00:01:26,180
Its name:

36
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:27,350
El Niño.

37
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,580
Unprecedentedly warm water in the central Pacific,

38
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:34,020
the strongest since records began.

39
00:01:34,930 --> 00:01:37,550
That heat changed the winds over the Atlantic,

40
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:42,830
turning them into shearing winds that tear storms apart before they grow.

41
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:46,190
The strongest winds of this kind since 1979.

42
00:01:46,190 --> 00:01:49,410
But the energy didn't disappear...

43
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:50,720
it just moved.

44
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:53,400
In the eastern and central Pacific:

45
00:01:53,910 --> 00:01:55,560
twenty-four storms,

46
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:57,570
thirteen of them hurricanes.

47
00:01:58,150 --> 00:01:59,760
A record season there...

48
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:01,820
and a record calm here.

49
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:03,210
Same cause.

50
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:04,900
And now the question:

51
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:10,380
experts say the 2027 season could be more active.

52
00:02:10,970 --> 00:02:13,000
So was this calm just a break...

53
00:02:13,390 --> 00:02:14,830
or the calm before the storm?

54
00:02:15,870 --> 00:02:17,170
Tell me what you think in the comments.

55
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:19,790
Full sources in the video description.

56
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:21,270
Subscribe to the channel,

57
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:24,370
and turn on the bell... so you don't miss the next trend.

